  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Pathways to Prosperity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Our Overall Goal is:
Producers and workers thrive by participating in supply chains that are economically profitable, socially responsible and environmentally sound.

The strategic objective of Pathways to Prosperity Programme is:

Contributing to the transformation of priority global and local supply chains by addressing systemic issues in an inclusive way in order to enable producers, MSME service providers and workers to thrive and generate more decent green jobs and thus reduce poverty and inequality.

In our Theory of Change we aim to contribute to the following 4 systemic outcomes in selected sectors and countries:
1. Viable and Resilient Production, in which capacitated producers have the knowledge and skills to grow and diversify production and businesses, in a climate-smart manner, offering more decent jobs to their workers (including rural youth, men and women);
2. Inclusive Service Delivery that ensure sustainable access to relevant technology, inputs, products and services,
tailored to the needs of farmers, miners, processors and agri-entrepreneurs);
3. Inclusive Finance that provide efficient, accessible and tailor-made financial services to producers and MSME service providers, with reduced risk;
4. Inclusive Market Connection, in which market actors adopt (and mainstream) inclusive trade, sourcing and pricing,
and reward policies and practices.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Pathways to Prosperity upscales the uptake of sustainable and inclusive production and trade practices across 16 countries and priority sectors worldwide. Connecting producers, service providers, financial institutions, buyers and governments, Solidaridad:
1. supports producers of key global commodities and local food crops to gain essential knowledge and entrepreneurial skills
2. creates fair and inclusive access to services and technology to improve production and trade
3. supports producers&apos; access to inclusive finance, and:
4. connects producers to markets.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Producers in the primary sector: self-employed farmers, rural entrepreneurs (men, women, youth), and
family businesses engaged in palm oil, cocoa, coffee, tea, cotton, and miners in gold

Producers in the secondary (industrial) sector: factories or tanneries focusing on textiles and leather, as
well as agri-processors (mills, factories).</narrative>
  </description>
